> > Dragonsbane: kills dragons instantly (or dragons always leave scales?)
> 
> Just the opposite: make it a mixed blessing: it kills them instantly, but
> because it slaughters them so thouroughly, it never leaves anything except
> the objects carried (no corpse, no scales).

That's a great idea! I kind of like the idea that others had mentioned
about "reflecting dragon breath", but the tradeoffs here are brilliant
for the middle game.  By the late game, you probably already have
scales or dragons are not an issue.

I was thinking the same sort of "effect" for trolls, where they just
sort of are killed so thoroughly that they leave no corpse and thus
can't regen.
 
> This would balance things out nicely. And you could also switch to another
> weapon if you really wanted those scales.

But then you'd have to actually fight the dragon.  ;)

> > Werebane: werefoo cannot summon help when wielded
> 
> I'd propagate making it work like a ring of protection from shape changers
> (but only for lycanthropes, not for other shape changers), and of course
> only when wielded. It should also give protection from lycanthropy (if
> this is not already part of protection from shape changers).

It already has protection from lycanthropy (which is really it's only
useful value right now).  That sounds like a good idea though.

> > Demonbane: does 2X damage against riders, as well (or more??)
> 
> I'm doubtful about this. I don't see the riders as real demons (no matter
> the fact that they are &), more as minor gods.

I can see your point.  I guess I just felt like Demonbane was a really
useless weapon given other double damage weapons that worked on the
same creatures.  Can you think of anything else that would help it be
more useful?
 
> > Tsurugi: always insta-kill engulfers
> 
> Why engulfers? Why should the chance suddenly be 100%? Would you want this
> for Vorpal Blade as well?

I was reading through the spoilers to make sure I covered all the
artifacts and the Tsurugi talked about having a chance to kill
engulfers.  I didn't look at the code for it, but maybe bisection
normally wouldn't work for engulfers.  I can't think of any large
engulfers that couldn't be bisected.

As for the 100%? Personally I think it should be really hard to miss
something that's engulfing you, especially with a giant artifact
sword.  It also makes it much more useful, both against air elementals
but also against vortices that you don't have resistances to.  I
figure that because it's a special case, maybe it's not too
unbalancing.
 
> > Grimtooth (I forget this even exists sometimes): always poisoned
> 
> I like this, even if it would introduce another weapon with a chance of
> instant-killing.

I thought it made for a great chaotic-themed weapon and potentially a
first rogue sacrifice.  Poison resistance is only a chance though, and
there are good amounts of poison resistant monsters.  I'm not sure
about balance issues exactly, but maybe a smaller chance (50%) of
being poisoned when attacking (followed by the chances that the poison
does anything).

> > Orcrist: 3X damage to orcs instead
> 
> That might be too unbalancing, since you can name Orcrist. Then again,
> anything that works against just a subclass of monsters is not easily too
> unbalanced.

There are always tradeoffs to naming artifacts.

I think Orcrist and Sting are really only good for the beginning of
the game. Sting has some other powers (web-cutting and orc-warning). 
I thought there should be a tradeoff between Sting and it's effects
and Orcrist with the better damage, but no warning.  I realize that it
does do about 3 more damage on average, but that didn't seem useful
enough.
 
> > I also propose a limit on artifacts found in the game to be a
> > predetermined random number from 3-6 (barring named, quest, and
> > invocation artifacts).
> 
> I wouldn't like this. Firstly, because I think the current artifact
> generation algorithms are already balanced enough, secondly because the
> changes proposed above aren't really that unbalancing themselves.

Maybe I never liked the idea of your god continuing to give you
artifact trinkets again and again and again.  I realize that they
become less likely to give you them, but given enough time you can get
all the (non-quest) artifacts in the game.    I like the idea of
either a hard cap on the number they'll give you or a harder soft cap,
such that they're much less likely to give you so many.  Maybe you're
right though and this shouldn't be forced on people.
 
> > Also, what about the idea of slightly random to-hit and damage
> > bonuses? That way you wouldn't know exactly how good each weapon is.
> 
> Please, no. The dX factor in damage calculation already makes for a
> randomised effect, which is in my opinion enough.

Looking back at that, I think it would be annoying (and tedious) to
the spoiled players and wouldn't change anything for the unspoiled
players.  It would also make it harder for people to play the game
unspoiled and have a grasp on which weapons were /actually/ better
than others.

